    {
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": "Public vs Private Health Insurance in Germany (2026)",
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Marco Maurelli", "url": "https://private-health-insurance-germany.com/about" },
      "dateModified": "2026-04-01",
      "inLanguage": "en"
    },
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      "itemListElement": [
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://private-health-insurance-germany.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Public vs Private Health Insurance", "item": "https://private-health-insurance-germany.com/compare" }
      ]
    }
  ]
};
---

<Layout
  title="Public vs Private Health Insurance in Germany — Compare Costs 2026"
  description="Private vs public health insurance in Germany for expats: a full comparison of costs, coverage, eligibility, family rules, and how to choose."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <main>
    <div class="page-wrap container">

      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <ol>
          <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
          <li aria-hidden="true">›</li>
          <li aria-current="page">Public vs Private Health Insurance</li>
        </ol>
      </nav>

      <header class="compare-header">
        <span class="page-tag">Comparison</span>
        <h1>Private vs Public Health Insurance in Germany: The Differences Explained</h1>
        <p class="page-lead">A full side-by-side breakdown of private vs public health insurance in Germany for expats. Costs, coverage, eligibility, family rules, and a clear decision framework.</p>
        <div class="meta-row">
          <span class="meta-item">By Marco Maurelli</span>
          <span class="meta-item">Updated April 2026</span>
          <span class="meta-item">15 min read</span>
        </div>
      </header>

      <div class="article-body">

        <p>Germany offers two parallel health insurance systems, and choosing between them is one of the most important financial decisions an expat makes. Get it right and you could save hundreds of euros per month while getting better coverage. Get it wrong and switching back is very difficult.</p>

        <h2>The Essential Difference</h2>

        <p>Public health insurance (<em>gesetzliche Krankenversicherung</em>) is Germany's statutory public health insurance. Contributions are calculated as a percentage of your gross salary, split between you and your employer. It covers everyone at the same rate regardless of age or health.</p>

        <p>Private health insurance (<em>private Krankenversicherung</em>) is private insurance where your premium is set by your age and health at entry, not by your income. Better coverage options are available, but each person in your household requires their own policy.</p>

        <h2>Cost Comparison</h2>

        <p>The financial case for or against private health insurance depends primarily on your income and age. Here is how the numbers compare for an employee in 2026:</p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Annual salary</th>
              <th>Public health insurance employee share/month</th>
              <th>Private health insurance at 32 (mid plan)/month</th>
              <th>Employee net difference</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>€80,000</td><td>~€548/month</td><td>€310/month (employer pays ~€310)</td><td>Private health insurance ~€238 cheaper</td></tr>
            <tr><td>€100,000</td><td>~€548/month (capped)</td><td>€310/month (employer pays ~€310)</td><td>Private health insurance ~€238 cheaper</td></tr>
            <tr><td>€120,000</td><td>~€548/month (capped)</td><td>€310/month (employer pays ~€310)</td><td>Private health insurance ~€238 cheaper</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="callout">
          <strong>Important context:</strong> public health insurance employee contributions are capped at the Beitragsbemessungsgrenze (€66,150/year in 2026, so ~€548/month employee share). For high earners, public health insurance caps at this level. Private health insurance premiums also have a ceiling, set by your plan choice. At salaries above ~€80k, the financial advantage of private health insurance for employees is roughly €150-300/month depending on plan and age.
        </div>

        <h2>Full Side-by-Side Comparison</h2>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Feature</th>
              <th>Public health insurance</th>
              <th>Private health insurance</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Premium basis</strong></td>
              <td>% of gross income (14.6% + supplement)</td>
              <td>Fixed by age and health at entry</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Family coverage</strong></td>
              <td>Free for non-earning spouse and children under 25</td>
              <td>Separate premium per person (including children)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Hospital room</strong></td>
              <td>Shared ward, assigned physician</td>
              <td>Private or semi-private room (plan dependent)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Choice of doctor</strong></td>
              <td>General ward physician</td>
              <td>Chief physician / senior consultant of your choice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Waiting times</strong></td>
              <td>Typically longer for specialists</td>
              <td>Generally faster access</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Dental coverage</strong></td>
              <td>Basic routine care only</td>
              <td>Comprehensive including implants (plan dependent)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Vision</strong></td>
              <td>Not covered for adults</td>
              <td>Covered in many plans (glasses/contacts)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Alternative medicine</strong></td>
              <td>Very limited</td>
              <td>Often included (osteopathy, acupuncture)</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Mental health</strong></td>
              <td>Covered, long waiting lists</td>
              <td>Covered, faster access</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Prescription drugs</strong></td>
              <td>Covered with co-pay</td>
              <td>Covered at reimbursement rate</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Who can join</strong></td>
              <td>Everyone (mandatory below threshold)</td>
              <td>Employees above €77,400, self-employed, civil servants</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Rejection risk</strong></td>
              <td>Cannot be rejected</td>
              <td>Pre-existing conditions can cause surcharges or exclusions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Employer contribution</strong></td>
              <td>~50% of total contribution</td>
              <td>Up to ~€421/month (half public health insurance equivalent)</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h2>Family Coverage: The Key Private Health Insurance Disadvantage</h2>

        <p>This is the most commonly underestimated factor when switching to private health insurance. In public health insurance, your non-earning spouse and children under 25 (and in education) are covered for free under your policy. A family of four costs the same as a single person in public health insurance.</p>

        <p>In private health insurance, every person requires their own contract and premium. A 35-year-old with a non-working spouse and two young children might face:</p>

        <ul>
          <li>Their own private health insurance: €420/month (employer pays ~€210)</li>
          <li>Spouse's private health insurance (age 34): €380/month (full cost, no employer contribution)</li>
          <li>Child 1 private health insurance: €100-130/month</li>
          <li>Child 2 private health insurance: €100-130/month</li>
          <li>Total family private health insurance: approximately €1,000-1,060/month</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Compare to the public health insurance family total: roughly €548/month (employee share only, family covered free).</p>

        <p>For families with children and a non-working spouse, private health insurance is often significantly more expensive, even at high incomes. Run the numbers carefully before switching if this applies to you.</p>

        <h2>Coverage Differences in Detail</h2>

        <h3>Dental</h3>

        <p>Public health insurance covers preventive checkups twice a year and basic extractions and fillings. Restorative work (crowns, bridges, implants) is only partially covered, often requiring large patient co-payments. Good dental work in Germany typically requires either private supplement insurance or significant out-of-pocket spending for public health insurance patients.</p>

        <p>Private health insurance plans with good dental coverage (typically 70-100% for major work) eliminate this issue entirely. Over a lifetime, the dental coverage difference can offset thousands of euros in out-of-pocket costs.</p>

        <h3>Mental Health</h3>

        <p>Both public and private health insurance cover psychotherapy and psychiatric treatment. The practical difference is access speed. Public health insurance patients typically wait 3-6 months for a psychological assessment and therapy slot. Private health insurance patients are private patients and generally find appointments within 1-2 weeks.</p>

        <h3>Specialist Access</h3>

        <p>Public health insurance patients often need a referral from their GP to see a specialist, and waiting times for initial specialist appointments can be months in busy cities. Private health insurance patients can self-refer and are generally prioritized in specialist scheduling as private-pay patients.</p>

        <h2>Switching Back from private health insurance to public health insurance</h2>

        <p>This is one of the most important points many guides overlook: <strong>returning to public health insurance after switching to private health insurance is genuinely difficult.</strong></p>

        <p>Employees can return to public health insurance only if:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Their income drops below the JAEG threshold for three consecutive calendar years</li>
          <li>They become unemployed and claim unemployment benefits (ALG I), which reinstates public health insurance enrollment automatically</li>
          <li>They retire (over 55 in some circumstances, but private health insurance remains the norm for most retirees)</li>
        </ul>

        <p>Self-employed people can return to public health insurance only if they become employees earning below the threshold. Simply having lower freelance income is not sufficient.</p>

        <p>The restriction exists because, without it, people would switch back to public health insurance when they become expensive to insure (older, sicker) and drain the public pool. As a result, switching to private health insurance should be considered a long-term, potentially permanent decision.</p>

        <h2>Who Should Choose private health insurance: Decision Framework</h2>

        <div class="decision-grid">
          <div class="decision-card decision-yes">
            <div class="decision-label">Private health insurance is likely better if you are:</div>
            <ul>
              <li>Single, no children, or all children are earning</li>
              <li>Earning above €90,000/year as an employee</li>
              <li>Self-employed at almost any income level</li>
              <li>A civil servant (with Beihilfe)</li>
              <li>Young and healthy (under 40)</li>
              <li>Planning to stay in Germany long-term</li>
              <li>Wanting faster specialist access and better hospital care</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="decision-card decision-no">
            <div class="decision-label">Public health insurance may be better if you are:</div>
            <ul>
              <li>Married with young children and a non-working spouse</li>
              <li>Earning close to the €77,400 threshold with income risk</li>
              <li>Over 45 with pre-existing conditions</li>
              <li>Planning to leave Germany within 2-3 years</li>
              <li>A KSK-registered artist or journalist</li>
              <li>Expecting your income to drop below the threshold</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2>How to Make the Decision</h2>

        <p>The most reliable approach is a personalized comparison. A licensed broker can calculate your exact public health insurance cost (based on your salary) and get real private health insurance quotes based on your age, health history, and coverage preferences. This takes the guesswork out of the comparison.</p>

        <p>The eligibility check takes 2 minutes and gives you an initial read on whether you qualify. The full broker consultation (free) gives you exact numbers.</p>

        <div class="cta-inline">
          <h3>Get a personalized public vs private health insurance comparison</h3>
          <p>A licensed broker will compare both options for your exact situation, for free.</p>
          <div class="cta-inline-btns">
            <a href="/eligibility-check" class="btn-primary">Take the eligibility check</a>
            <a href="/contact" class="btn-outline">Speak with a broker</a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>

